---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
import CodeCard from '../components/CodeCard.astro';

const initCmd = `nuthatch init 0xA0b86991c6218b36c1D19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48 \\
  --alias usdc --chain mainnet`;

const rawQuery = `SELECT block_number, tx_hash, log_index, "from", "to", value
FROM usdc__transfer
ORDER BY block_number DESC, log_index DESC
LIMIT 10;`;

const httpQuery = `curl -G 'http://127.0.0.1:8288/sql' \\
  --data-urlencode 'q=SELECT count(*) AS transfers FROM usdc__transfer'`;
---
<Layout
  path="/example"
  title="From contract to nest: indexing USDC - Nuthatch"
  description="A worked Nuthatch example: index USDC from its contract address, inspect raw event tables, and choose an authored view or maintained entity. Runs on Nuthatch v5.14.1."
>
  <section class="page">
    <article class="wrap narrow prose">
      <p class="section-label">Worked example</p>
      <h1>From contract to nest: indexing USDC</h1>

      <p class="preview-note">
        <strong>This is the current, executable path on v5.14.1.</strong> It creates a nest from
        a verified contract, backfills its recent history, follows the chain, and serves decoded event data locally.
        The data surface is read-only SQL over HTTP and MCP. GraphQL is a partial read surface, exact
        for a subgraph's event-shaped fields and a named refusal for the rest, it is parked, and the
        default binary has no GraphQL route: it is served only by the <code>nuthatch-graph</code>
        download attached to every release since 4.11.0. See the <a href="/roadmap">roadmap</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nuthatch begins with a contract, not a hosted indexing service. This example indexes USDC
        on Ethereum mainnet. The same shape works for a contract of your own, or for a published
        nest checked out with <code>nuthatch init --from &lt;git-url&gt;</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>One command, one nest</h2>
      <CodeCard code={initCmd} lang="bash" label="shell" />
      <p>
        <code>init</code> vendors the ABI, records the chain and contract in
        <code>nuthatch.toml</code>, and writes the schema and authoring files. The explicit
        <code>--alias usdc</code> gives the event tables stable, readable names.
      </p>
      <p>
        USDC is a proxy, so <code>init</code> also warns that events before block 18,921,498, when the
        current implementation was deployed, will not decode under this scaffold. Add the earlier ABI
        as a second contract entry before trusting historical totals.
      </p>
      <CopyBlock command="nuthatch dev --backfill 300" />
      <p>
        The process backfills the last 300 blocks, so there are rows within seconds, follows the tip,
        and serves a local API at <code>http://127.0.0.1:8288</code>. Without <code>--backfill</code>,
        <code>dev</code> backfills from the contract's deployment block, which for USDC is 20 million
        blocks and wants your own archive-capable RPC rather than the free public defaults.
      </p>

      <h2>Queryable before you model anything</h2>
      <p>
        Every declared event becomes a table named <code>{'{'}alias{'}'}__{'{'}event{'}'}</code>.
        For this nest, Transfers are <code>usdc__transfer</code>. The raw layer includes provenance
        such as block number, transaction hash, and log index alongside the decoded event fields.
      </p>
      <CodeCard code={rawQuery} lang="sql" label="the ten latest USDC transfers" />
      <p>
        Start with <code>GET /schema</code> or <code>GET /tables</code> when you do not know a
        nest’s shape. Solidity’s <code>from</code> and <code>to</code> are SQL reserved words, so
        they need double quotes in a query.
      </p>

      <h2>Serve the data</h2>
      <p>
        The SQL endpoint is deliberately small: it accepts read-only <code>SELECT</code> or
        <code>WITH</code> queries as the <code>q</code> query parameter. It is a GET endpoint, not a
        Postgres wire protocol and not GraphQL.
      </p>
      <CodeCard code={httpQuery} lang="bash" label="query over HTTP" />
      <p>
        The response covers both sealed historical segments and the live hot tip. Query limits,
        timeouts, and read-only enforcement are part of the endpoint, not an optional client habit.
        A reverse proxy should still provide authentication and per-client rate limits before exposing
        it publicly.
      </p>

      <h2>Turn facts into your application model</h2>
      <p>
        Raw events are available immediately. Add an authored view when your application needs a
        stable derived shape, and commit checks with recorded fixtures when the result matters. A
        view is evaluated at query time, so it is broad and inexpensive until someone asks for it.
      </p>

      <h2>When a view becomes a hot read</h2>
      <p>
        If one grouped answer is asked often enough that rescanning history is the expensive part,
        make the distinction explicit: put its one <code>SELECT</code> in
        <code>entities/holders.sql</code> and declare its key and bound in
        <code>entities.toml</code>. Nuthatch then maintains the relation for each arriving block,
        exposes it to <code>/sql</code> by name, and retracts it on a reorganisation.
      </p>
      <CodeCard code={`[[entities]]\nname = "holders"\nsql = "entities/holders.sql"\nkey = ["holder"]\nmax_rows = 100000`} lang="bash" label="entities.toml" />
      <p>
        This is not a faster spelling of a view. An entity reserves memory and accepts a deliberately
        small SQL subset, but makes a repeated aggregate an indexing-time cost instead of a query-time
        one. Keep general SQL in <code>views/</code>. The full authoring contract, including restart
        seeding and the admissible SQL, is in the <a href="/docs/build/entities/">entities guide</a>.
      </p>

      <hr class="divider close-div" />
      <p class="signoff">
        The result is a self-hosted, replayable event index you can query without a deploy key or a
        gateway. For the full command and HTTP reference, continue to the
        <a href="/docs/start/quickstart/">quickstart</a> or the <a href="/docs/reference/http-api/">HTTP API</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) clamp(4rem, 9vw, 6.5rem); }
  .prose h1 { margin-bottom: 1.6rem; line-height: 1.12; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 2.8rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.5rem; }
  .prose p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.72; margin-bottom: 1.3rem; }
  .prose p:first-of-type { color: var(--text); }
  .preview-note { background: var(--pale-rust-bg); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 3px solid var(--rust); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1rem 1.2rem; font-size: 0.95rem !important; color: var(--text-muted) !important; }
  .preview-note strong { color: var(--text); }
  .prose :global(.copyblock), .prose :global(.codecard) { margin-bottom: 1.3rem; }
  .close-div { margin-block: 2.4rem 1.8rem; }
  .signoff { color: var(--text) !important; }
</style>
